Z||000017||ToddAsh||12-16-2001||02:56 PM||||You said your using 10W30 and were thinking of going to 40W! 10W40 is STILL 10W oil.It just protects like 40W when warmed up.You need to get the first # higher.A 20W50 is fine in Pa in the winter.No Fiero should ever use a 5Wanything oil.Too thin for the old 4 or V6.In lower mileage motors a 10W30 or 40 is fine.Older motors need a thicker oil.If you have any different weights of oil at home,try pouring them into a container and notice the differences.All 10s are the same. Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000018||theogre||12-16-2001||05:01 PM||||Item 1.... whenever you suspect low oil pressure use another gauge to test it. NEVER trust a dash gauge!<P>Item 2.... When the dash gauges don't give a constant reading, ESPECIALLY when the reading changes with variations in electrical system loads... Check all the gound straps and battery cables. read the wire service article in my cave. A single bad ground tie can easily screw up the dash gauges. This is why ECM sensors nearly all have their own power and ground ties.||||reg||1
